**Read:** [Cleanroom programming and energy — Tschudi, Sartor and Xu](../sources/Cleanroom-Programming-and-Energy-LBNL.lingua#r_36R5RS6HEWBTYN4SHCVPQNP12X), mechanical air systems; [Multizone airflow and contaminants — Dols and Polidoro, CONTAM 3.4](../sources/CONTAM-Multizone-Airflow-and-Contaminants-NIST.lingua#r_5HECDC480RSYAJ3SQ2QNFHCN46), zones, airflow paths and air-handling systems.

Supply enters the room; return leaves for a system that may recirculate part of it. Makeup replaces air lost through exhaust or outward leakage. Recirculation can remove a specified contaminant through filtration without replacing the same volume with outdoor air.

Draw every split and recombination. At steady density, room inflow equals outflow; recirculation appears on both sides of the room boundary. A filter rating does not establish that recirculation is suitable for every process emission.

**Work:** Balance a room receiving 5 m³/s, returning 4 m³/s to a recirculation branch and exhausting 1 m³/s. Show the needed makeup and its treatment. Add a leakage path and rebalance the system. Explain how a changed exhaust demand can affect room pressure even if recirculating fan flow is unchanged.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
